          "documents": [
            {
              "type": "ED Notes",
              "title": "Emergency Department Record (outside)",
              "date": "2025-05-12T08:30",
              "author": "emergency medicine",
              "body": "OUTSIDE ED RECORD, received by fax 5/12/2025. Date of service 5/10/2025 to 5/11/2025.\n\nCC: witnessed seizure at workplace.\n\nHPI: 35 yo M arriving by EMS from his office. Coworkers describe him slumping at his desk and then shaking all four limbs for roughly two minutes. Afterward he was confused and very sleepy for a while; mental status improving on arrival. On questioning he admits to a few earlier spells over the years that he brushed off and never had evaluated. Has not been sick recently. Denies drug or alcohol use. No headache, no recent head injury.\n\nPMH: none. Meds: none. Allergies: NKDA.\nFH: father with epilepsy. SH: office administrator, never smoker, no ETOH.\n\nExam: sleepy but wakes to voice, follows commands, oriented by the second hour. Lateral tongue bruised/bitten. Small superficial laceration inner lower lip, edges well apposed, cleaned, no repair needed. VS stable throughout. Neuro: PERRL, no facial asymmetry, moves all extremities symmetrically, no drift, reflexes symmetric, no focal deficit.\n\nResults: glucose 98, Na 140, Ca 9.2, rest of CMP unremarkable. CBC normal. Prolactin drawn about 20 minutes post-event: 44 ng/mL (high).\nEEG (obtained in observation the next morning): intermittent temporal sharp waves, no seizures captured.\n\nCourse: placed in ED observation for EEG. No recurrent events overnight. Discussed with on-call neurology by phone: recurrent unprovoked spells plus epileptiform EEG meet criteria for epilepsy; agreed with starting valproic acid.\n\nDx: Epilepsy, new diagnosis. Tongue bite and minor lip laceration.\n\nDisposition: discharged home with roommate 5/11. Valproic acid 500 mg PO BID, Rx sent. State law reviewed: no driving until six months seizure-free; seizure precautions (no swimming alone, no heights, showers rather than baths). Outpatient neurology referral; PCP follow-up this week. Return for any further seizure, a seizure longer than 5 minutes, or not waking up between seizures."
            }
