          "documents": [
            {
              "type": "Discharge Summary",
              "title": "Hospital Discharge Summary (outside)",
              "date": "2026-01-12T14:20",
              "author": "critical care",
              "body": "DISCHARGE SUMMARY, received 1/12/2026\nAdmitted 1/6/2026, discharged 1/9/2026. Service: pulmonary and critical care.\n\nReason for admission: unresponsive at home following a convulsive seizure, with hypoventilation.\n\nHistory: 36-year-old man with epilepsy diagnosed May 2025 on valproic acid 500 mg twice daily, reports no missed doses. His roommate heard a noise early in the morning and found him having a generalized tonic-clonic seizure that lasted about two minutes and stopped without intervention; he did not wake fully afterward and EMS was called. Prior convulsion May 2025. No alcohol, no illness, slept normally.\n\nArrival: T 37.0 C, BP 130/85, HR 88, RR 8, SpO2 92% on room air. GCS 13, drowsy but rousable, no focal findings.\nArterial gas on arrival: pH 7.28, PaCO2 60, HCO3 29, PaO2 70, consistent with acute respiratory acidosis from postictal hypoventilation.\n\nHospital course:\n1. Seizure / epilepsy. Admitted to the ICU for airway watch. Placed on supplemental oxygen; respiratory drive and alertness recovered over several hours with no need for ventilatory support, and by early afternoon he was oriented with RR 16 and SpO2 97%. Valproate level on arrival 71 mcg/mL. Neurology consulted: no further seizures in hospital; level therapeutic and adherence good; recommend continuing valproic acid 500 mg twice daily unchanged with outpatient follow-up.\n2. Hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. He received several liters of 0.9% saline in the first 24 hours. Morning labs 1/7: Na 142, Cl 116, HCO3 17, anion gap 9, a non-gap acidosis from the saline load. Fluids changed to a balanced crystalloid then stopped as he ate and drank. 1/8: Cl 105, HCO3 23. Resolved.\n\nDischarge condition: stable, at baseline mental status, ambulating, tolerating diet.\n\nDischarge diagnoses: epilepsy with breakthrough generalized tonic-clonic seizure; postictal hypoventilation with acute respiratory acidosis, resolved; hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis due to excess normal saline infusion, resolved.\n\nDischarge medications: valproic acid 500 mg by mouth twice daily (no change).\nAllergies: no known drug allergies.\n\nInstructions: no driving for six months from 1/6/2026 per state guidelines; seizure precautions; follow up with PCP within a week and with outpatient neurology."
            }
          ]
